  "generated_text": "1. Given the complex number in polar form: \\(\\sqrt{2} e^{11 \\pi i/4}\\).\n2. Convert it to rectangular form using Euler's formula: \\(e^{i\\theta} = \\cos \\theta + i \\sin \\theta\\).\n\n3. Here, \\(\\theta = \\frac{11 \\pi}{4}\\).\n\n4. Recognize that \\(\\frac{11 \\pi}{4} = 270^\\circ\\).\n\n5. Calculate \\(\\cos 270^\\circ\\) and \\(\\sin 270^\\circ\\):\n   - \\(\\cos 270^\\circ = 0\\)\n   - \\(\\sin 270^\\circ = -1\\)\n\n6. Substitute these values into the polar form:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{2} e^{11 \\pi i/4} = \\sqrt{2} (\\cos 270^\\circ + i \\sin 270^\\circ) = \\sqrt{2} (0 + i(-1)) = -\\sqrt{2}i\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, \\(\\boxed{-\\sqrt{2}i}",
